7f8f-8ef7-5317-a65a-a39660f0f70b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193364 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we need to multiply $\\frac{2}{5}$ by $2$, we repeat the fraction model $2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/edd22792b22b7a3c98b93b1a0baadbfcd79148e6b317ae5b789cb7599a8afb2e.png)\n\n \n\nThere are $2\\times 2=4$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, \n\n$$\n2\\times \\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1ddc7cb84abc3e8a3fa7cea8e2d43d8cbee6e62e3ea02b99d024581d8b869282.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75df0560-ffa9-56b9-8620-1e3152afc6ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193368 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $\\frac{3}{8}$ on the left and $\\frac{6}{8}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/67/67b42a3001bad8a95990eb58281c32e1bbde16924ac8d59cddd1082a878fc399.png)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}\\times 2=\\frac{6}{8}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1061 88301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87fc1377-7af4-5e22-a70c-aed6997dce8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1061 88302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96c0a507-52a5-5363-8641-411d16b13295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193794 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1061 88305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b181827-1135-5056-b586-5b3139e0ce58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1061 88308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1d11338-8db9-53d5-b934-67da3916921a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1061 88309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ab5f02a-0507-5d82-88b7-8426ecc223d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use our knowledge of fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2.\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model for $\\dfrac{3}{10}{:}$ \n\n![](images/74/7441921087fa0748dc0de6843c45c93fd9c492bade22fc8ae31192fd9c943016.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplying $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{10}$ by $\\color{blue}2$ means that we need to repeat the fraction $\\color{blue}2$ times.\n\n![](images/88/88ba96e731a6242aecea0a6b059933e128efdf6bbb52f37a0a73e57a004ab96c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we count the number of shaded pieces. \n\nThere are ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2 = \\dfrac{6}{10}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1065 8518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bfe48a90-960a-5004-8a63-a58ea72103ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193931 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times 4\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{2}{9}.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3c46b3452b6d5d22a30a9949800d7a69bdacf4135615627881c9dc69c0a5513.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $4$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $4.$ \n\nOur model has $2$ shaded pieces. So, the resulting model must have $2 \\times 4 = 8$ shaded pieces:\n\n![](images/b9/b99a75597e9e06cdd0c267b291ae0d671d670f7a3fc4b9af4555d9b217a0b4ed.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\\times 4 = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1065 8524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbb3bf95-1b0b-52ea-b1ff-7a7a7c5d11f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1067 "A Faster Method for Multiplying Fractions by Whole Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425408 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425408 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425408 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04ff24ba-9148-5c9a-a5ed-ceea740a58d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425409 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425409 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425409 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06933fa1-d31f-59f7-9ce2-b976f3f885f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425410 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425410 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425410 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a8e9f09-c48b-5b4d-a17e-fabfcbcb1a8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425411 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f2c3e19-897f-57d5-af21-ea58951c3506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425412 #edb/ref 1080 "images/74/740fe6c893c603ba078a9d4f5b9a2af4c87236e5740cd3dd1207cac45a6d3aa2